<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
      Deliberately NO <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
      anywhere in this file, and none of the security/data layer's dependencies
      (Room, androidx.security-crypto, androidx.biometric, Kotlin coroutines)
      request it either. Without INTERNET, the OS sandbox makes this
      application PROCESS structurally incapable of opening a socket at all —
      not "configured not to," but unable to, the same guarantee the web app's
      "no network call ever carries financial data" promise (CONTRACTS.md §5)
      rests on. Do not add this permission for any reason without revisiting
      that promise explicitly with the user first.
    -->

    <!--
      allowBackup="false", and this is the single most important attribute in
      this file after the absence of INTERNET.

      With it true (the platform default, and what this file said until now)
      Android Auto Backup silently copies the app's private data directory -
      which is the whole encrypted vault - to the user's Google Drive, on the
      OS's own schedule, with no prompt. The app never makes the upload, so
      having no INTERNET permission does not prevent it: the operating system
      does it on the app's behalf. That would have quietly undone the promise
      the rest of this design is built around, and it would have done it
      without a single line of code being wrong.

      The vault is AES-256-GCM under a PBKDF2-600k key, so this was never
      plaintext leaving the phone. It is still the wrong default here: the
      user asked for their financial history to stay on their device, a
      copy in cloud storage is a copy an attacker can attempt offline at
      leisure, and the honest answer to "where does my data live" has to
      stay "on the phone, and nowhere else".

      Backups are the user's to make deliberately - Settings has an explicit
      .tally export they control and can put wherever they choose.

      android:extractNativeLibs and the rest are left at defaults; only the
      data-egress-relevant flags are set here.
    -->
    <application
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:fullBackupContent="false"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:theme="@android:style/Theme.Material.Light.NoActionBar">

        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/app_name">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
          Notification-listener service (capture/**, per ANDROID.md §3, CONTRACTS.md
          §5). Two things make this bind at all:
            1. `android:permission="android.permission.BIND_NOTIFICATION_LISTENER_SERVICE"`
               is a system-only permission; only NotificationManager itself can
               bind a service declaring it, which is what makes `exported="true"`
               safe here (required for the system to bind it at all) rather than
               a security hole.
            2. The intent-filter action
               `android.service.notification.NotificationListenerService`:
               this exact string is how the OS discovers, on the "Notification
               access" settings screen, that this app offers a listener to grant
               access to. Get either of these two wrong and the service simply
               never binds; no crash, no error, it just silently never
               receives a notification, so both are copied verbatim from the
               platform's documented contract rather than approximated.
          No `android:process` override: this runs in the app's main process,
          not a separate one, since it shares the encrypted buffer with the
          rest of the app via a plain SharedPreferences file scoped to this
          package, not IPC.

          NOTE (UI agent, android/**/ui/** owner): this comment previously had
          stray double-hyphen runs, invalid inside XML comments and enough to
          fail manifest merging; fixed in place here without touching the
          surrounding service/intent declaration below. Flag to the capture
          agent if the wording needs revisiting.
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".capture.ingest.CaptureNotificationListenerService"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="Tally capture"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_NOTIFICATION_LISTENER_SERVICE">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.service.notification.NotificationListenerService" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

    </application>

</manifest>
